# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET loader integration built on the Intropy framework.
A generic-host worker consumes a Dapr pub/sub topic through a streaming
subscription (the app connects to its sidecar; it serves no HTTP). Each
message is rebuilt into a CloudEvent, run through the Intropy loader pipeline,
and written as `{orderId}.json` through a Dapr destination binding.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- App type: generic-host worker (long-running; a Deployment in production)
- No app port and no HTTP endpoints: the framework's loader host
  (`AddLoader`, `Intropy.Framework.Hosting`) opens a Dapr streaming
  subscription and sends each message's ack (Success / Retry) back over it
- Health: a pipeline that cannot be composed stops the host with exit code 1;
  a broken subscription stream is reopened. There is no HTTP probe
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Pub/sub component: `pubsub` — provided by the system host; messages are
  published from there
- Message: `{{ .subscribes }}` — the CloudEvents type the pipeline consumes; the publishing extractor uses the same message identity
- Topic: `{{ .channel }}`; the topic and the subscription's messages and filters are the `topic` and `routes` in `.intropy/scaffold.json`, rendered by the system host as the component's Dapr Subscription. The code names neither the pub/sub nor the topic: the loader takes what the Subscription delivers
- Destination port: `{{ .port }}` — the named interface the loader
  writes the outside world through; the system host declares it in
  `Ports.cs` and the Dapr binding uses the same name, resolved to a local
  folder in development. In production the same name resolves to the
  environment's binding (e.g. SFTP) from the deploy overlay's GitOps
  values — rebinding the port is a deployment change, never a code change
- Idempotency Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Business Incident Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Shared contracts: the consumed payload type (`{{ .payloadType }}`) — derived from the message name — lives in the
  sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}` (namespace `{{ .contracts }}`), referenced
  from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. The sibling is scaffolded by this template's
  dependency declaration; when it already exists (another component created
  it) the scaffold leaves it untouched. The destination load record (`Out`)
  stays local in `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `Taskfile.yml` - build and test tasks (`task build`, `task test`; the
  suites also run separately via `task test:unit` / `task test:integration`).
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the chiseled ASP.NET Core
  runtime, which the framework's hosting package needs; the app listens on no
  port (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .` —
  the shared contracts sibling enters as a named context); the image contains
  only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment
  environment.
- `src/Program.cs` - host bootstrap: builds the host from
  `Composition.CreateHostBuilder` and runs it until SIGTERM.
- `src/Composition/Composition.cs` - the host builder and every service
  registration, ending in the single-pipeline `AddLoader` overload and its
  unrouted policy. The sender is a DI-registered
  `SendStep<Out, Context>` resolved by `WithSenderFromServices()` like any other
  external. Note the namespace/type collision: `{{ .name }}.Composition` is both
  namespace and class, so call sites inside nested namespaces use the fully
  qualified `Composition.Composition.CreateHostBuilder(...)`.
- `src/Constants.cs` - the component name, the destination port, and the
  CloudEvent context keys the idempotency step
  reads (root namespace — consumed by both `src` and the tests).
- `src/Routes/{{ pascal .subscribes }}/` - the loader's typed pipeline steps
  (`Deserializer`, `Validator`, `Transformer`, `Sender`) and message identity.
  All DI and `AddLoader` wiring lives in `src/Composition/`.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/` - pipeline step unit tests (pure xUnit; no
  fakes except the Sender's `IFileAdapter` seam via NSubstitute, no Dapr;
  `task test:unit`).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - delivery, boot-smoke, and
  composition tests against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes, sharing the
  `LoaderSut` host fixture (`task test:integration`; `task coverage` reports
  branch coverage).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json` (copied to the build output), overridden by
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables — the generic host reads
them from the binary directory (its content root) and `Composition` reads the
platform-service app ids from `PlatformServices:*`. Deployment overlays the file
via ConfigMap. Components that need typed options bind them themselves.

The file adapter behind each port is chosen by `Ports:{{ .port }}`: `Kind` selects the Dapr
binding the port is deployed with (`Local`, `Sftp` or `AzureBlob`). `src/appsettings.Development.json`
selects `Local`, the local folder the system host resolves each port to (the host runs
every component as Development). `appsettings.json` sets no `Kind`: a deployment sets
`Ports__<port>__Kind` to match the binding it renders, and without it the component
fails at startup naming the port.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this loader runs via its system host,
which provides every Dapr component (pub/sub, destination binding, platform
services) and carries the sample data used to publish a message. The
component-level loop is build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

`task test` runs both test projects (`task test:unit` / `task test:integration`
run them separately): the unit suite pins each pipeline step's own contract;
the integration suite starts the real host (`LoaderSut`, built from
`Composition.CreateHostBuilder`) with only the externals faked
(`InMemoryFileAdapter` at the keyed destination seam,
`FakeIdempotencyServiceClient`, `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient` — from
`Intropy.Framework.Testing`), delivers CloudEvents over the loader's
subscription exactly as the sidecar would (`FakeStreamingSubscriber`), and asserts
on fake state and the delivery ack. `task coverage` runs the integration
suite with branch coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's
`TestResults/`).

## Development notes

- The destination `IFileAdapter` is registered (from configuration, see above) as a keyed singleton under its port name
  (`Constants.DestinationPort`). Inject it via
  `[FromKeyedServices(...)]` or resolve via `GetRequiredKeyedService<IFileAdapter>(...)`.
- Loader idempotency keys off CloudEvent `Subject` (id) and `Time` (date),
  read from the context keys `cloudevent.subject` / `cloudevent.time`
  (constants in `Constants.cs`, mirroring the framework's internal keys —
  there are no id/date extractor lambdas to configure).
- Ack mapping (framework-owned): success, an idempotent duplicate, and a
  routed business incident all ack Success (redelivering a validation failure
  would loop forever); technical failures and interrupted messages ack Retry
  and the sidecar redelivers.
- Redelivery detection: the sidecar marks redeliveries (`retrycount`), which
  reach the pipeline context as `IsRetry` — a successful redelivery resolves
  the business incident an earlier delivery routed.
- Additional extraction steps go into the builder chain in
  `src/Composition/Composition.cs` (the `AddLoader` builder chain) via `.WithExtractor(...)` —
  the loader pipeline supports any number of them, executed in order after
  deserialization.
{{- if .empty }}
- The step bodies are scaffolded empty (`NotImplementedException`). The
  idempotency and business-incident wiring stays in place — `LoaderBuilder`
  requires both. There are no idempotency lambdas to stub; instead, stamp
  the `cloudevent.subject` / `cloudevent.time` context keys from a stable
  business identifier and business timestamp in your `Deserializer` — a
  per-delivery value (fresh GUID, wall-clock time, or the `dapr publish`
  envelope's delivery-time `time`) makes the check pass everything.
- The test suite is real and fails out of the box by design — `task test` is
  red until the steps land (only `CompositionTests` and the boot smoke test
  start green). The pinned behaviors flip green one by one as the
  implementation progresses; no test edits are needed to get there. Two
  delivery tests from the full sample suite are deliberately not scaffolded:
  business-failure incident routing (which input fails validation is the
  Validator's to define; scaffolded now its assertions would hold vacuously),
  and duplicate-ack (the duplicate simulation relies on the idempotency
  commit the implemented pipeline records). Add them as the corresponding
  implementation lands, copying the full sample suite's versions.
{{- else }}
- The `Deserializer` stamps the `cloudevent.subject` / `cloudevent.time`
  context keys from the order's `OrderId` / `OrderDate` — for
  extractor-published events these equal the envelope values, and a
  `dapr publish` envelope carries no subject and a wall-clock time. Keep the
  stamping pointed at *business* fields — a per-delivery value (fresh GUID,
  wall-clock time) makes the idempotency check pass everything.
- Business failures (validation errors) are routed to the Business Incident
  Service, keyed on the order id (falling back to the envelope subject).
{{- end }}

